{"uid":"9ffac3f4e9700f57","name":"test_a_pending_carved_follow_up_still_faces_the_ordinary_ladder","fullName":"tests.test_worker_hat#test_a_pending_carved_follow_up_still_faces_the_ordinary_ladder","historyId":"9b517b27744358b328157dc4045bae36","time":{"start":1791930551859,"stop":1791930552161,"duration":302},"description":"Companion to the test above (task 003, #166): the new direct-to-review\n    shortcut fires ONLY when nothing else in the plan is actionable\n    (`_unresolved_failures`). Here the carved follow-up, 002, is left\n    `pending` instead of `completed` -- a genuinely outstanding task the\n    scheduler still has work to hand out for -- so it is THIS iteration's\n    `assigned_id` (task 116's own scheduler: a pending task with no unmet\n    dependency is always picked over nothing), which makes the very same\n    `plan_complete` claim structurally invalid (`_apply_worker_plan_\n    complete_report`'s \"only valid when no task was assigned\" check, task\n    015) rather than caught by the new shortcut -- the shortcut is gated on\n    `assigned_id is None` and never even runs `_unresolved_failures` when a\n    task was assigned. So this claim reaches `contract_enforcement.\n    enforce_contract`'s ordinary re-ask ladder exactly as it did before\n    task 003, unrepaired every pass (002 stays `pending` forever -- the\n    scripted worker only ever repeats the same illegal claim) -- and, since\n    this is the (default, `max_approaches=1`) LAST approach, three such\n    no-progress passes pause the run and escalate rather than route it to\n    review, exactly like `test_a_plan_with_work_left_is_never_routed_to_\n    review` above pins for its own (already-`failed`-vs-`pending`) shape.","descriptionHtml":"<p>Companion to the test above (task 003, #166): the new direct-to-review<br />\nshortcut fires ONLY when nothing else in the plan is actionable<br />\n(<code>_unresolved_failures</code>). Here the carved follow-up, 002, is left<br />\n<code>pending</code> instead of <code>completed</code> -- a genuinely outstanding task the<br />\nscheduler still has work to hand out for -- so it is THIS iteration's<br />\n<code>assigned_id</code> (task 116's own scheduler: a pending task with no unmet<br />\ndependency is always picked over nothing), which makes the very same<br />\n<code>plan_complete</code> claim structurally invalid (<code>_apply_worker_plan_ complete_report</code>'s &quot;only valid when no task was assigned&quot; check, task<br />\n015) rather than caught by the new shortcut -- the shortcut is gated on<br />\n<code>assigned_id is None</code> and never even runs <code>_unresolved_failures</code> when a<br />\ntask was assigned. So this claim reaches <code>contract_enforcement. enforce_contract</code>'s ordinary re-ask ladder exactly as it did before<br />\ntask 003, unrepaired every pass (002 stays <code>pending</code> forever -- the<br />\nscripted worker only ever repeats the same illegal claim) -- and, since<br />\nthis is the (default, <code>max_approaches=1</code>) LAST approach, three such<br />\nno-progress passes pause the run and escalate rather than route it to<br />\nreview, exactly like <code>test_a_plan_with_work_left_is_never_routed_to_ review</code> above pins for its own (already-<code>failed</code>-vs-<code>pending</code>) shape.</p>\n","status":"passed","flaky":false,"newFailed":false,"newBroken":false,"newPassed":false,"retriesCount":0,"retriesStatusChange":false,"beforeStages":[{"name":"event_loop_policy","time":{"start":1791930548331,"stop":1791930548332,"duration":1},"status":"passed","steps":[],"attachments":[],"parameters":[],"shouldDisplayMessage":false,"stepsCount":0,"attachmentsCount":0,"hasContent":false,"attachmentStep":false},{"name":"tmp_path_factory","time":{"start":1791930548332,"stop":1791930548332,"duration":0},"status":"passed","steps":[],"attachments":[],"parameters":[],"shouldDisplayMessage":false,"stepsCount":0,"attachmentsCount":0,"hasContent":false,"attachmentStep":fal